WEBVTT

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:47.900
And welcome to Doctor Who, the Hartnell Years, a celebration of the era of Doctor Who that

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.980
started what was to become an internationally popular series and very much a part of British

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:53.400
folklore.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.120
This is a nostalgic look at some of the early years of Doctor Who before decimalisation,

00:01:59.380 --> 00:01:59.980
wheel clamps...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.500
Colour TV and ghetto blasters.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.910
The BBC Film and Videotape Library at Brentford, a few miles from here,

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.990
houses many hundreds of thousands of programmes.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.480
And during the 70s, a mass sought-out took place to make room for other recordings.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.420
Some early Doctor Who episodes were retained, however,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.120
as examples of different types of stories.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.420
But sadly, some were destroyed.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.960
By the 70s, the sales potential had been fully exploited commercially

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.040
and there were various problems with regard to contract negotiations

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.980
for transmissions...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.920
of vintage material. This is not now the case, and so we decided to share with you

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.760
some of the Hartnell material which hasn't been shown since its original

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:53.020
transmission. But first things first. William Hartnell was born on 8th of

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.940
January 1908. His first stage appearance was in 1924 and he first

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.980
entered films in 1931.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
He also made innumerable TV appearances, including The Army Game, Crimes of Passion, and of course, Doctor Who.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.600
I'm here at the BBC Enterprises Exhibition Store in West London.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:23.070
Now, as you can see, I'm surrounded by costumes, monsters, special props, scripts, and, good gracious, a ghetto blaster.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.980
This one was used by my companion Ace in a story called Remembrance of the Dalek.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.170
Now, this is an Aladdin's cave of memorabilia stretching back nearly 30 years.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:43.640
William Hartnell, or Billy as he was known to his friends, created the role of Doctor Who.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:49.430
And the Doctor's first appearance on British TV took place on the 23rd of November 1963,

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.310
the day after President Kennedy's assassination.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.400
Two months earlier, on September the 27th, a trial run was given to the first episode.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.700
It was never transmitted.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.420
Let's take a look at what was really the first episode of Doctor Who.

