01:01:55.970 --> 01:01:58.830
I couldn't have put it better myself.

01:02:00.250 --> 01:02:03.830
And now, an example of one of William Hartnell's latest adventures coming up.

01:02:04.520 --> 01:02:09.420
It's the final episode of a four-part story by Brian Hales called The Celestial Toymaker.

01:02:10.090 --> 01:02:15.170
Now, once again, this is the only remaining episode of this story regarded by many as a classic adventure.

01:02:15.620 --> 01:02:23.380
It was originally transmitted in April 1966 and concerns the TARDIS crew's survival in the domain of the Toymaker,

01:02:23.690 --> 01:02:26.390
who dominates an evil fantasy world.

01:02:26.880 --> 01:02:29.400
Michael Goff, as always, is superb as the Toymaker.

01:02:30.090 --> 01:02:33.270
And by this time, all the Doctor's original companions have departed.

01:02:33.890 --> 01:02:37.990
Now, on board with the Doctor now are Stephen Taylor, played by Peter Purvis,

01:02:38.270 --> 01:02:40.430
and Dodo Chaplet, played by Jackie Lane.

01:02:41.190 --> 01:02:46.430
Many of the trilogic game sequences involved the use of a double for the Doctor's hands

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:50.670
in order to lighten William Hartnell's workload in The Final Test.

01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:24.840
Hurry.

01:03:25.480 --> 01:03:27.020
And where's the next game we have to play?

01:03:27.560 --> 01:03:28.000
Yaroo!

01:03:28.450 --> 01:03:29.550
Right over here!

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:34.480
You won't find it so easy this time, you know.

01:03:34.920 --> 01:03:37.990
Because, you see, you'll be playing against me.

01:03:40.940 --> 01:03:55.410
Here we are. Here's the next game.

01:03:56.320 --> 01:03:57.200
Tardis hopscotch.

01:03:57.840 --> 01:03:59.280
What are we supposed to do with it?

01:03:59.420 --> 01:03:59.960
Well, it's very...

01:04:00.560 --> 01:04:05.180
So each player takes one of these dice and throws them onto the triangle.

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:09.490
And the number that you've thrown will appear up at the end there, on the dice indicator.

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:12.310
That's to make absolutely certain that no one can cheat.

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:14.780
And you move forward with the number that you've thrown.

01:04:15.060 --> 01:04:19.100
The first one to reach triangle 14 is the winner. Easy, isn't it?

01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:21.950
Too easy. What's the catch?

01:04:22.360 --> 01:04:24.600
No catch. The first one home is the winner.

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:26.740
That gives us a two-to-one chance over you.

01:04:27.160 --> 01:04:28.260
Why, so it does.

01:04:29.570 --> 01:04:29.970
Well...

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:34.780
There you are. You just jump. From triangle to triangle, the first one home wins.

01:04:35.080 --> 01:04:36.000
Why do I have to jump?

01:04:36.480 --> 01:04:40.160
Well, you can walk if you like, but I shouldn't if I were you.

01:04:40.710 --> 01:04:41.310
Why not?

01:04:41.680 --> 01:04:48.330
Well, you see, all the area between the triangles is electric. You'll be killed.

01:04:52.060 --> 01:04:55.220
I don't think your friends will do so well now, Doctor.

01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:59.450
Cyril hates to lose, so he makes sure that he never dies.

01:05:00.920 --> 01:05:03.080
Oh, don't stop playing, Doctor.

01:05:03.700 --> 01:05:05.020
You're so near the end.

01:05:05.940 --> 01:05:09.340
Soon you'll discover whether or not you've got the sequence right.

01:05:16.820 --> 01:05:17.860
I think you need help.

01:05:18.850 --> 01:05:19.510
Go for a move.

01:05:19.710 --> 01:05:21.190
Nine hundred and thirty.

01:05:39.280 --> 01:05:43.940
At this rate, you'll finish your game long before Stephen and Dodo find the TARDIS.

01:05:44.660 --> 01:05:50.170
It will be fun having you here, and your friends will make charming dolls.

01:05:51.260 --> 01:05:56.690
It may interest you to know that their chairs are ready and waiting for you in the doll.

01:06:08.410 --> 01:06:10.190
Now, you all know what we have to do.

01:06:10.900 --> 01:06:13.620
Yes, ladies first, if you don't mind.

01:06:22.470 --> 01:06:29.120
Three, one, two...

01:06:32.170 --> 01:06:32.430
Three.

01:06:32.610 --> 01:06:34.430
Ah, you're next.

01:06:36.340 --> 01:06:38.080
You're just too good to be true, aren't you?

01:06:43.710 --> 01:06:44.010
Four.

01:06:48.380 --> 01:06:57.790
One, two, three, four.

01:06:59.720 --> 01:06:59.980
Says...

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:01.790
Go on to number seven, do I?

01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:03.670
Yes, go on.

01:07:04.930 --> 01:07:05.310
Go on.

01:07:07.170 --> 01:07:09.990
Oh, by the way, I should have told you.

01:07:10.410 --> 01:07:12.990
If you land on a triangle that's already occupied,

01:07:13.550 --> 01:07:16.310
the first player has to go back to the starting post.

01:07:17.240 --> 01:07:19.260
Now I see why you let us go first.

01:07:20.640 --> 01:07:22.280
All right, go on, don't keep us in suspense.

01:07:22.640 --> 01:07:27.430
We're all part of the game, chap, too.

01:07:31.630 --> 01:07:35.800
Now, it's my turn. I think I'm going to enjoy this game.

01:07:38.810 --> 01:07:39.090
Three.

01:07:40.820 --> 01:07:44.660
Oh, I'm sorry, Stephen. Do I have to send you back to the start?

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:48.100
Look, surely. We can't send each other back. We're supposed to be playing together.

01:07:48.340 --> 01:07:51.760
If you don't abide by the rules, you can't possibly win the game.

01:07:52.650 --> 01:07:57.250
Right, Stephen. We must play fair.

01:07:59.390 --> 01:07:59.970
After all...

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:00.980
Well, we are two to one.

